1
00:00:07,350 --> 00:00:12,308
Last time we proved the improvement lemma by walking forward from day to day.

2
00:00:12,308 --> 00:00:17,432
Once a job has made a candidate an offer, she holds that job or a better one.

3
00:00:17,432 --> 00:00:22,048
That is true at the end of that day and of every later day.

4
00:00:22,216 --> 00:00:24,990
Here is a different way to prove the same thing.

5
00:00:24,990 --> 00:00:28,063
On the day of the offer itself the claim is true,

6
00:00:28,063 --> 00:00:32,326
because she keeps the best of her offers and this job is among them.

7
00:00:32,326 --> 00:00:35,198
So let us paint that day green.

8
00:00:35,350 --> 00:00:37,920
Now suppose the lemma were false.

9
00:00:37,920 --> 00:00:44,158
Then on some later days the claim fails, so let us paint those days red, wherever they may be.

10
00:00:44,158 --> 00:00:47,784
Among the red days, look at the first one.

11
00:00:47,950 --> 00:00:52,670
The first red day is not the day of the offer, because that day is green.

12
00:00:52,670 --> 00:00:59,096
So there is a day just before it, and that day is not red, because then ours would not be the first.

13
00:00:59,096 --> 00:01:02,606
The claim is true on the day before.

14
00:01:02,783 --> 00:01:07,901
So on the day before, she holds a job at least as good as the original one.

15
00:01:07,901 --> 00:01:13,679
She did not refuse that job, so by the step from last time it asks her again on the red day.

16
00:01:13,679 --> 00:01:19,437
Then she keeps it or something better, which makes the claim true on the red day.

17
00:01:19,616 --> 00:01:24,986
But a day cannot be red and green at once, so the first red day does not exist.

18
00:01:24,986 --> 00:01:29,160
And if there is no first red day, there are no red days at all.

19
00:01:29,160 --> 00:01:33,452
So the lemma holds, and we have proved it a second time.

20
00:01:35,416 --> 00:01:37,538
Is this a new kind of proof?

21
00:01:37,538 --> 00:01:40,924
Both proofs begin by checking the day of the offer.

22
00:01:40,924 --> 00:01:48,158
And both do the same work in the middle, which is to show that a green day is never followed by a red one.

23
00:01:48,316 --> 00:01:55,668
Induction tells it forwards, where green today forces green tomorrow, so the green runs on forever.

24
00:01:55,668 --> 00:02:01,960
The second proof tells it backwards, where a first red day would need a green day right before it.

25
00:02:01,960 --> 00:02:05,768
It is one argument told in two directions.

26
00:02:07,750 --> 00:02:11,366
But the backward telling leaned on something we never proved.

27
00:02:11,366 --> 00:02:14,130
We said, look at the first red day.

28
00:02:14,130 --> 00:02:17,698
Why should a set of days have a first one at all?

29
00:02:17,850 --> 00:02:22,018
Days are counted with natural numbers, so let us try it with numbers.

30
00:02:22,018 --> 00:02:26,272
Take the set five, two, eleven, seven and eight.

31
00:02:26,272 --> 00:02:32,728
Its smallest element is two, and in a finite set we can always find it by comparing.

32
00:02:32,883 --> 00:02:35,401
Infinite sets work just as well.

33
00:02:35,401 --> 00:02:38,989
The odd numbers never end, but they start at one.

34
00:02:38,989 --> 00:02:43,047
The primes never end, but they start at two.

35
00:02:43,216 --> 00:02:45,358
Here is why it cannot fail.

36
00:02:45,358 --> 00:02:52,196
Pick any element of the set, say eleven, and only finitely many natural numbers lie below it.

37
00:02:52,196 --> 00:02:58,506
So we can check them one by one, and the lowest one that belongs to the set is the smallest.

38
00:02:58,683 --> 00:03:02,065
This fact is called the well ordering principle.

39
00:03:02,065 --> 00:03:06,859
Every set of natural numbers that is not empty has a smallest element.

40
00:03:06,859 --> 00:03:12,496
Our red days were such a set, not empty because we supposed the lemma false,

41
00:03:12,496 --> 00:03:15,167
and so a first red day had to exist.

42
00:03:17,150 --> 00:03:19,836
Other number systems are not so kind.

43
00:03:19,836 --> 00:03:25,712
Take the integers, which include the negative numbers, and look at the set of all negative ones.

44
00:03:25,712 --> 00:03:34,128
Below minus one comes minus two, then minus three, and it never ends, so this set has no smallest element.

45
00:03:34,283 --> 00:03:38,443
The real numbers contain that same set, so they fail too.

46
00:03:38,443 --> 00:03:42,021
What if we only allow zero and everything above it?

47
00:03:42,021 --> 00:03:45,037
Then look at the set of all positive numbers.

48
00:03:45,037 --> 00:03:51,165
Whatever positive number you call the smallest, half of it is smaller and still positive.

49
00:03:51,316 --> 00:03:56,912
Zero would be below them all, but zero is not positive, so it is not in the set.

50
00:03:56,912 --> 00:04:03,480
So first counterexamples belong to the natural numbers, and to things counted by them, like our days.

51
00:04:03,480 --> 00:04:10,058
Next we use the improvement lemma to show that the result of propose and reject is stable.

